{% sec 1, "What a Prime Broker Is", "Who does all the work behind a hedge fund?", "What a prime broker is" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine opening a restaurant with just a chef and two cooks. You don't build your own kitchen, run your own farm or hire your own accountant. You rent a fully equipped kitchen, a supplier delivers ingredients on credit, and someone else does the books and the deliveries. You focus on the cooking.</p>
    <p>A hedge fund works the same way. A portfolio manager, a few analysts and a trader focus on picking positions. Almost everything else comes from a {% gl "prime broker" %}prime broker{% endgl %} (PB): a division of a big investment bank that lends the fund money, lends it shares to short, holds its assets, settles its trades and reports on its risk.</p>
    <p>You've already met the PB in this chapter: it's the stock-loan desk the execution trader calls for a locate, and the lender that issues the margin calls. This note looks at it from the bank's side, and at what happens when the relationship goes wrong.</p>
    <h3>Who the prime brokers are</h3>
    <p>A handful of banks dominate: Goldman Sachs, Morgan Stanley and JPMorgan serve most of the world's hedge fund assets, followed by banks such as BofA, Barclays, BNP Paribas, UBS and Deutsche Bank. They compete hard for funds because prime brokerage pays steady, recurring revenue: the fund pays interest, borrow fees and commissions every day it trades, whether markets go up or down.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>The prime broker decides how much a fund can borrow and what it can short. The most important thing about it isn't the price; it's whether it stands by you when markets go wrong.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "The Services", "What does a prime broker actually do for a fund?", "The services" %}
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>What it is</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>{% gl "custody" %}Custody{% endgl %}</td><td>Safekeeping the fund's cash, shares and other assets in separate accounts</td><td>Assets kept apart from the bank's own are protected if the bank fails (in theory; see section 5)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Margin financing</td><td>Lending the fund money to buy more than its own capital</td><td>Charged at a {% gl "financing spread" %}spread{% endgl %} over a benchmark rate such as SOFR: often SOFR \(+\,35\) to \(75\) bp</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Securities lending</td><td>Finding shares for the fund to borrow and short</td><td>Access to hard-to-borrow shares can decide whether a short is possible at all</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Execution</td><td>Trading through the bank's desks and algos</td><td>Many funds trade through their PB to keep the relationship (and revenue) in one place</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "capital introduction" %}Capital introduction{% endgl %}</td><td>Introducing the fund to investors such as pension funds, family offices and endowments</td><td>No direct fee, but the PB expects trading business in return. Valuable for new funds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Operations</td><td>Settling trades, processing dividends and other corporate actions, cash management, reporting</td><td>Lets a fund run with a small back office</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Risk and analytics</td><td>Real-time exposure, stress tests and factor analysis (for example Goldman's Marquee, Morgan Stanley's Matrix)</td><td>For smaller funds, this can replace a risk system of their own</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Synthetic prime brokerage</td><td>Giving the fund exposure through swaps instead of owning the shares</td><td>More leverage and easier access to some markets (section 6)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Inside the fund, the PB relationship touches almost everyone. The head trader manages it day to day, the execution trader gets locates from its stock-loan desk, operations reconcile cash and collateral with it every morning, and the CFO negotiates its rates. Roles at the PB itself (financing, stock loan, client service, capital introduction) are a common way into hedge fund careers from the sell side.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "How a Prime Broker Makes Money", "What does a fund pay, and for what?", "How it makes money" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Prime broker revenue</span>\[ \text{revenue} = \text{financing spread} + \text{borrow markup} + \text{commissions} + \text{minimum revenue top-up} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Financing spread.</strong> The PB borrows money cheaply and lends it to the fund at a spread above the benchmark rate. A large fund might pay SOFR \(+\,35\) bp; a small one SOFR \(+\,75\) bp or more.</li>
      <li><strong>Borrow markup.</strong> The PB borrows shares from lenders and lends them on to the fund at a higher fee, keeping the difference. Often it lends shares straight out of other clients' accounts, which is cheaper still.</li>
      <li><strong>Commissions</strong> on trades the fund sends through the PB's desks.</li>
      <li><strong>Minimum revenue guarantees.</strong> Smaller funds often promise the PB a minimum amount of business a year. If the fund trades less, it pays the difference anyway.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Reusing your collateral</h3>
    <p>There's a hidden part of the business. When a fund borrows from its PB, it usually lets the PB reuse the shares it holds as collateral: lend them to other clients for shorting, or pledge them to raise cash. This is called {% gl "rehypothecation" %}rehypothecation{% endgl %}, and it's a big reason the PB can lend cheaply. In the US, a PB can reuse client securities worth up to \(140\%\) of what the client owes it. The catch comes if the PB fails: shares it has reused are no longer sitting in your account (section 5).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>What does a prime broker earn from a fund?</h3><p>Set the fund's size, gross and net exposure. The numbers are illustrative: a financing spread from \(75\) bp (small funds) down to \(35\) bp (large), a \(0.3\%\) borrow markup on shorts, \(2\) bp commission with the book traded four times a year, and a \(\$300{,}000\) minimum.</p></div>
