{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "Choosing a Prime Broker", "If you were launching a fund, what would you look for?", "Choosing a prime broker" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For a new fund launching with \(\$50\) to \(\$200\) million, picking a PB is one of the biggest early decisions. It sets how much the fund can borrow, which shares it can short, the systems it runs on, and often which investors it meets. The terms are agreed at launch and renegotiated as the fund grows; bigger funds that trade more get cheaper financing, lower borrow markups and better commissions.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>What to check</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Balance sheet strength</td><td>A PB that pulls your financing in a crisis can force you to sell at the bottom. Will it stand by you through a drawdown?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Borrow inventory</td><td>How many shares it can lend, especially hard-to-borrow names. Critical for long/short equity funds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Technology and reporting</td><td>Real-time risk, reports and data feeds that plug into the fund's own systems</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total cost</td><td>Financing, borrow, commissions <em>and</em> minimums together, not just the headline rate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Counterparty risk</td><td>Could the PB itself fail and trap your assets?</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Service</td><td>A team that answers the phone at 6am in a crisis is worth paying for</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The most important point is the first one. In calm markets every PB looks similar. The difference shows when markets fall: that's exactly when a PB can raise margin requirements, cut how much it will lend, or recall borrowed shares, and a fund with a nervous PB can be forced to sell everything at the worst moment.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "When the Prime Broker Is the Risk", "What happens if your prime broker goes bust?", "When the PB is the risk" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Funds worry about their PB pulling financing. In 2008 they learned to worry about something worse: the PB itself failing.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bear Stearns, March 2008.</strong> Hedge funds feared for Bear and moved their cash and positions to other banks within days. The money running out of its prime brokerage helped push Bear into an emergency sale to JPMorgan.</li>
      <li><strong>Lehman Brothers, September 2008.</strong> When Lehman collapsed, hedge funds with accounts at its London business found their assets frozen in the bankruptcy. Shares Lehman had reused (rehypothecated) were gone from their accounts; the funds were left as creditors waiting in line. Some waited years to get assets back.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After Lehman, most institutional funds moved to {% gl "multi-prime" %}multi-prime{% endgl %}: two or three prime brokers, with strict rules on keeping assets separate and limits on how much each PB can reuse. Running several PBs costs more and is more work, but it's insurance: if one fails or turns nervous, most of the fund keeps working.</p>
    <p>The risk runs both ways. In 2021 Archegos traded with several PBs at once, and none of them could see the full size of its positions. When it failed, the banks that sold first lost little and the slowest lost billions (the Margin and Leverage note has the story). Credit Suisse, the biggest loser, later handed its prime brokerage clients to BNP Paribas.</p>
  </div>
