{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "Synthetic Prime Brokerage", "Can a fund get a position without owning anything?", "Synthetic prime brokerage" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>With a normal ("physical") account, the fund owns the shares, held for it at the PB. With synthetic prime brokerage, the fund owns a contract instead: a {% gl "total return swap" %}total return swap{% endgl %}. The bank pays the fund any rise in the share plus dividends; the fund pays the bank any fall plus a financing fee. The bank usually buys the shares itself to hedge, so the fund gets the same gains and losses without owning anything.</p>
    <p>Why would a fund want that?</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>More leverage.<span class="why">A swap isn't a share purchase, so rules like Reg T's \(50\%\) don't apply. The bank sets the margin itself.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>Access.<span class="why">Some markets are hard or slow for foreign funds to enter directly. The bank already has the access, so the fund trades the swap.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>Simpler operations and tax.<span class="why">No settlement in each local market, and in some countries different tax on dividends.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>Less visible.<span class="why">The fund doesn't own the shares, so in many cases it doesn't have to disclose a large stake. This is exactly what let Archegos build huge positions that no single bank could see, and why regulators have since pushed for more reporting of swaps.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The trade-off is counterparty risk: the fund's gains are only as good as the bank's promise to pay them.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "Is there prime brokerage in my three markets?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX prime brokerage</h3>
    <p>In FX, each trade is a promise between two parties to swap currencies later, so every bank needs to trust whoever it trades with. A fund can't open a credit line with twenty banks. Instead, it gets one with an FX prime broker. The fund then trades with any bank on the PB's list, and each trade is {% gl "give-up" %}given up{% endgl %} to the PB, which steps in as the other side for the bank. The banks trust the PB; only the PB has to trust the fund.</p>
    <p>FX PB had its own Lehman moment on 15 January 2015, when the Swiss National Bank suddenly dropped its cap on the franc. The franc jumped about \(30\%\) against the euro in minutes, many clients' losses went past their whole deposit, and some FX brokers couldn't cover their clients' losses. FXCM, a large retail broker, needed a \(\$300\) million rescue loan, and Alpari UK went bust. FX prime brokers then cut back on smaller clients and asked the rest for more margin.</p>
    <h3>Gold: allocated or unallocated?</h3>
    <p>Bullion banks play a similar role for gold. The key choice is the type of account. <strong>Allocated</strong> gold is specific bars held in your name; if the bank fails, they're yours. <strong>Unallocated</strong> gold is a promise from the bank to deliver gold, like a bank deposit: cheaper, but if the bank fails you're a creditor. It's the same custody question as rehypothecation. For gold futures, the equivalent of a PB is the clearing broker; when the futures broker MF Global collapsed in 2011, about \(\$1.6\) billion of client money was missing and clients waited months to get it back.</p>
    <h3>Bonds: the basis trade</h3>
    <p>For bond funds, the PB (and the dealers' repo desks) finance positions through repo. One of the largest hedge fund trades runs this way: the Treasury "basis trade", buying government bonds and selling bond futures against them to capture a tiny price gap, made worthwhile by borrowing \(20\) to \(50\) times or more. In March 2020, when funding got tight and margins rose, funds had to unwind these trades at once, and the US Federal Reserve stepped in to buy Treasuries to calm the market.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>PB</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Prime broker, or prime brokerage.</p><p class="eg">"Our PB's cutting financing on small caps."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Financing / the spread</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The loan from the PB, and how much above the benchmark rate it costs.</p><p class="eg">"We got financing down to SOFR plus 40."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Stock loan</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The PB desk that finds shares to borrow.</p><p class="eg">"Ask stock loan if they can find any."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Cap intro</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Capital introduction: the PB introducing funds to investors.</p><p class="eg">"Cap intro set up six meetings with family offices."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Multi-prime</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Using several prime brokers to spread the risk.</p><p class="eg">"We're multi-prime: Goldman, MS and JPM."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Rehypothecation</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The PB reusing a client's collateral for its own lending and funding.</p><p class="eg">"Cap the rehyp in the new agreement."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Synthetic / swap</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Holding a position through a total return swap instead of owning it.</p><p class="eg">"We hold the Asian book on swap."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Give-up</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>In FX, handing a trade done with one bank over to your prime broker.</p><p class="eg">"Trade with whoever's best, it all gets given up."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where the prime broker idea turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: your broker is your mini prime broker</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A retail trading platform does a small version of a PB's job: it holds your money, lends you leverage and settles your trades. So the same question applies: what happens if it fails? In the UK, regulated firms must keep client money separate from their own, and the FSCS protects up to \(\text{£}85{,}000\) if an investment firm collapses. An offshore broker may offer neither. With a prop firm, you don't own the account at all: the firm does, which is why its rules can close you out.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: the lender that stands by you</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A property developer's main bank plays the PB role: it lends, sets the terms, and can decide whether to stand by a project when sales slow. Choosing that lender has the same lessons as choosing a PB. The cheapest headline rate matters less than whether it will extend a loan in a downturn, and spreading big projects across more than one lender stops one nervous bank from forcing a sale.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: who's really holding your assets?</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Nigeria has no prime brokerage industry: local funds rarely borrow or short, and use custodian banks to hold their assets. Foreign funds reach Nigerian shares through global custodians, or through swaps and notes from international banks. When foreign investors sold Nigerian shares in 2016 and again in 2022–23, many couldn't change the naira back into dollars for months. Their money wasn't lost, but it was frozen: the same lesson as a failed PB. Assets you can't move when you need them aren't really liquid.</p>
      <p>Apps that let Nigerians buy US shares usually hold them through a US partner broker, so your shares are only as safe as each link in that chain keeps client assets separate. Making that chain clear to users is a real fintech opportunity.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you put numbers on a prime broker relationship?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Financing cost to the fund</span>\[ \text{cost} = \text{loan} \times (\text{SOFR} + \text{spread}) \times \frac{\text{days}}{360} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Longs and shorts from gross and net (as % of the fund)</span>\[ \text{longs} = \frac{G + N}{2} \qquad \text{shorts} = \frac{G - N}{2} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">US rehypothecation limit</span>\[ \text{securities the PB may reuse} \le 140\% \times \text{what the client owes} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Exposure to one PB failing (split evenly across \(n\) PBs)</span>\[ \text{assets at risk} = \frac{1}{n} \times \text{total assets} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>US money markets count interest on a \(360\)-day year, which is why the formula divides by \(360\), not \(365\).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>A \(\$200\) million fund runs \(200\%\) gross and \(40\%\) net, borrowing what it needs from its PB at SOFR \(+\,60\) bp.</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Longs \(= \frac{200\% + 40\%}{2} = 120\%\), or \(\$240\) million. Shorts \(= 80\%\), or \(\$160\) million.</li>
      <li>The fund has \(\$200\) million of its own, so it borrows about \(\$40\) million to pay for the longs (short sale proceeds are held as collateral, so they don't pay for longs).</li>
      <li>The PB's spread: \(\$40\text{m} \times 0.6\% = \$240{,}000\) a year.</li>
      <li>Borrow markup on \(\$160\) million of shorts at \(0.3\%\): \(\$480{,}000\) a year.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">For a long/short fund, lending shares can earn the PB more than lending money.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

