      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The \(\$180{,}000\) of normal charges plus a \(\$120{,}000\) top-up.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>A fund splits its assets evenly across three PBs and one fails. What share of its assets is still usable straight away?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Two thirds. With only one PB, it would have been none.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>Why might a fund hold a position through a total return swap instead of owning the shares?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>More leverage (no Reg T limit), access to hard-to-reach markets, simpler settlement and sometimes tax, and often no need to disclose a large stake. The cost: it relies on the bank to pay, and the opacity can hide dangerous size.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>Interview question: you're launching a \(\$100\) million long/short equity fund. What do you look for in a prime broker?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>First, whether it will stand by the fund in a drawdown: balance sheet strength and its record in past crises. Then borrow inventory for the shorts, total cost including minimums, technology and reporting, capital introduction to help raise money, and service. Plan for a second PB as the fund grows, to spread counterparty risk.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
